this epic trench battle from 1917  was shot to look like one long take and  it was a huge  challenge for both the cast and crew it  involved multiple cranes  speeding trucks and perfectly timed  handoffs  but this is just one example of a  one-take scene or a oner  that pushed filmmakers to their limits  and required  impressive technical innovations here's  how eight movie and tv scenes were shot  to look like one long tape  one take scenes are naturally suited for  intense action  as you can see in this fight scene in  atomic blonde  despite shooting in a real building the  crew still had to alter the set to  accommodate the wanter  for example they built this elevator  which helped create a natural stitch  point  a way to hide the moment the camera cuts  and keep the continuous illusion going  they also use another stitching  technique called body crossing  in which a cut is hidden when a  performer blocks the camera as seen here  with this henchman  because the fight was shot to look like  one take it needed to be shot in  chronological order  so the effects team had to constantly  apply new makeup to charlize theron and  the other characters as the fighting  continued and  injuries increased charlize did her own  stunts for a large part of this sequence  however for some of the riskier moves  the crew subbed in her stunt devil  monique ganderton  for example the director used motion  blur when charlize had to fall down the  stairs and slam into a wall  that's actually monique tumbling down  the stairs before switching back to  charlize hitting the wall  unlike atomic blonde netflix's  extraction hops from location to  location  as opposed to just one building the  entire sequence goes on for about 12  minutes and consists of approximately 36  segments cobbled together  the non-stop action required director  sam hargrave to do some stunts of his  own  for this car chase he strapped himself  to the hood of another vehicle  and then had to quickly unstrap himself  and place the camera inside the other  car  he then had to replicate the same shot  backwards by leaning out the car once he  was inside  this helped create a natural transition  point if sam's position was off even  slightly  they'd have to shoot it all over again  then for this shot where chris hemsworth  stunt double tumbles off an awning  sam roped himself into a pulley system  to get up close to the action  the sequence also fools the eye in many  other ways for instance when ovi runs  through this doorway he is actually  running into a room in a different  building 10 blocks away  the crew had to change locations to one  where there was more space to pull off  the upcoming action  and like with atomic blonde they  frequently swapped out chris with his  stunt double  bobby hall and hanton the first shot you  see here is  chris's face they could then pan to his  double without anyone noticing by  shooting the back of his head  for the day of the dead parade scene  inspector cinematographer hoytavon  hoitima  wanted to start big and then go small  and intimate  that meant starting wide with parade  shots then getting in closer to the  marchers before following bond into a  hotel room  to pull this off hoyte used a  technocrane the crane started high and  then lowered down into the crowd without  cutting  a steadicam operator then jumped off  pushed farther into the crowd  and followed actor daniel craig up to  the hotel room  while the street scenes were shot in  mexico the hotel room was actually  set in the uk the crowd scenes featured  1500 extras according to ilm vfx  supervisor mark bukowski  since they couldn't get a perfect shot  where every extra looked the right way  at the right time the vfx team inserted  digital  masks over their faces  sometimes runners are limited by the  space they're shot in  which means the crew needs to be a bit  more creative  in the season 2 premiere of the  marvelous mrs maisel  cinematographer m david mullen had to  lead the camera through a fictional  department store  through a mail slot and then into the  small room where midge mazel works as a  switchboard operator  unlike other continuous shots there were  no wide spaces for the camera to pass  through  just five feet of space between these  two rows of actresses  for both camera person and performer it  was about precise  timing the space was so tight that the  actresses had to lean back so they  wouldn't get hit by the camera  then when the camera went through the  center of the room actresses wheeled  around in their chairs so they wouldn't  block the shot  for birdman it wasn't just one scene but  the entire movie that needed to look  like one long take  according to director alejandro gonzalez  and yarito  some of the takes were up to 20 minutes  long because of the amount of movement  in the film  birdman was shot using small handheld  cameras and steadicams  these allowed the crew to more easily  follow the cast as they walked through  new york's crowded streets  this also meant the crew couldn't use  typical large light kits  so according to cinematographer emmanuel  lubezki  they instead relied on natural and  practical lights in the scene  like these colorful light bulbs above  michael keaton and even the lights  emitting from times square  birdman wasn't just a challenge for the  crew it was also a huge endeavor for the  cast  according to emma stone if she flubbed  even a single word  she'd have to start again from the very  top  like birdman 1917 was also filmed to  look like  one single take over its entire two-hour  running time  but it was even more complicated due to  its epic battle sequences  pulling this film off involved the most  careful planning imaginable  every set was precisely measured out and  could be no longer than the length the  camera could travel  since the crew was constantly on foot  they relied almost entirely on natural  light  look at the scene where scofield runs  through a destroyed city  it was lit up by flares in the sky  because the flares were the main light  for part of the scene  the crew needed to time exactly how long  the flares needed to be in the air  they figured this out in pre-production  where they ran a light above a mini  model of the set  to see where exactly all the shadows  would fall of course the film also  relied on stitch points  like here when blake and scofield walk  through a black curtain  but most of its magic was accomplished  using innovations in technology  for example schofield's journey down  river was captured by a drone  meanwhile lighter and smaller cameras  allowed the crew to move through  trenches and difficult terrain with ease  then to get across no man's land  cinematographer roger deakins and the  crew attached cameras to a cable system  above the set  this shot where scofield runs through a  destroyed city and jumps off a ledge and  into a river  was too dangerous to do in real life so  the part of him running was shot on set  while the jump was actually shot using  cgi with a swap background that allowed  for a seamless transition  shooting continuous takes means it's  hard to call cut so it's not ideal for  making mistakes  but this moment here where actor george  makai runs alongside the trench and then  trips  was not intentional but in the end it  felt natural  so it stayed in the movie  the entire set for this episode of the  haunting of hill house had to be built  specifically to accommodate a single  shot  take a look at this elevator it was  designed to lower the camera person  down to the next floor and allow them to  capture the shot of olivia and hugh  running down the stairs without a break  because the camera goes between hill  house and the funeral home in the  episode  the two stages were actually built  adjacent to each other and connected by  a hallway  this allowed the camera to follow hugh  as he walked directly from the funeral  home  into the house runners are frantic by  nature  and they involve a lot of movement and  setup changes the hill house set  had places throughout for the crew and  their equipment to hide whenever the  camera turned in their direction  like with birdman and 1917 hill house  also demonstrated the challenges of  lighting a warner  if an actor missed a cue they wouldn't  be lit properly so  series creator mike flanagan came up  with a unique solution  when he clapped these two wood blocks  together the cast member knew what to do  one take scenes aren't just used to show  movement from place to place  they can also be really useful in  showing the passage of time  this scene from the showtime series  kidding shows the character  shayna watching mr pickles puppet time  on her tv  as her life progresses and improves  around her  the shot captures five years of her life  over the course of one minute and 42  seconds  because the camera had to circle  constantly around the room as it changed  this endeavor was an absolute mad rush  for the cast and crew  to show the passage of time the camera  operator circled around the room  each area of the room would show a new  development in shaina's life  so whenever the camera wasn't focused on  them the 50 crew members had to swap out  furniture and bring in new props  then this wall slid over into the  kitchen while that was happening actress  ricky lindholm had to go through  multiple costume changes  some of which involved using a body  double  what are some of your favorite one take  scenes let us know in the comments  [Music]
 

